node_modules/
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
dist/
build/
src/generated/
.DS_Store
*.log
# Everything under .factory/ EXCEPT the locks. Written as an exclusion of the
# CONTENTS (`.factory/*`) rather than the directory (`.factory/`), because git
# cannot re-include a file whose parent directory is itself excluded: the
# walker never descends into it and the `!` rule is never consulted. Getting
# that wrong leaves floor.json ignored while the .gitignore reads as though it
# is tracked, which is exactly the kind of silent gap this repository keeps
# finding.
#
# The whole of .factory/ was previously ignored, not just its subdirectories,
# so `.factory/needs-human.md` and `.factory/locks-runtime/` were
# untracked-but-stageable and a blind `git add -A` would have published factory
# internals to a public repo. That still holds for everything except the locks.
.factory/*
# The locks must be TRACKED. A build runs in a `git worktree`, which contains
# only tracked files, so an ignored floor.json does not exist where the gate
# looks for it and every run prints FLOOR_ABSENT. The ratchet then guards
# nothing while appearing to be configured, which is the worst state for a
# safety property to be in.
#
# They carry no secrets: they are integers a human chose. They are on
# factory/guard.py's protected list, so a pull request that lowers one is
# auto-rejected. Tracking them is what makes that protection reachable.
!.factory/locks/
.factory/locks/*
!.factory/locks/*.json
# decisions.md must be TRACKED for the same reason as the locks: two node
# prompts (plan.md, triage.md) tell a builder to read it BEFORE escalating, and
# a build runs in a worktree that contains only tracked files. Ignored, the file
# is absent exactly where it is consulted, so every node escalates questions
# that were already answered and a human gets woken for a decision they already
# made.
#
# It carries decisions and their rationale, no secrets.
!.factory/decisions.md
*.pem
*.key

# Scratch briefs written while driving the build. They carry local tooling
# paths, and they are working notes rather than product content. Named as a
# pattern so a new one is covered on the day it is created, not after it leaks.
.brief-*.md

# MISSION.md WAS ignored here, on the assumption it was a working note.
# It is not: it is the specification for a public product, CONTRIBUTING.md
# tells contributors to read it first, and the validator reads it from the
# base branch so a pull request cannot edit the rules it is judged by.
# Ignoring it broke that read and pointed contributors at a file they could
# not see. It is tracked now, and it has been leak-scanned.
CLAUDE.md
factory/

# Licensed stock assets. Usable ON the site, never redistributed IN the repo.
# MotionElements royalty-free forbids giving the file away, and a public repo does exactly that.
mockups/assets/*.mp4
mockups/assets/*.webm
public/assets/*.mp4

# The factory lives in the private ops repo and is symlinked in so it can
# operate on this tree. It must never be committed here.
factory

# FACTORY_RULES.md WAS ignored with the factory code, which conflated two
# different things. The factory's SOURCE is operational tooling and stays
# private. The RULES it is bound by are governance for a public project, and
# the validator reads them from the base branch so a pull request cannot edit
# the rules it is judged against. Ignoring them broke that read, exactly as it
# did for MISSION.md. Tracked now, with internal tooling paths redacted.

# Named explicitly because the factory preflight checks the RULE, not the file.
# A git add -A must never be able to publish a secrets file that appears later.
secrets.json
credentials.json
*.pem
*.key

# Factory build worktrees. Every build runs in .worktrees/<slug>/, which is a
# complete second copy of the project living inside the main checkout.
.worktrees/

# Factory reports are scratch, never deliverables. A build once committed
# final-report.md to the repo root; it quoted absolute paths while describing
# its own work and the publish gate blocked the branch. The prompt says to
# write reports to the run directory; this makes the mistake unstageable.
report.md
final-report.md
*-report.md
